Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  jubilation and emotion as the first  passengers touched down in sydney and  reunite with their families a ban on  international travel was lifted on  monday after more than 18 months and now  fully vaccinated australians are  arriving without having to quarantine  it's a pretty emotional day today um we  lost my father-in-law last week so  it's happy feelings and sad feelings all  at the same time but so glad to see our  family finally sheer  gut-wrenching sadness of why i'm here  uh  but you know you can't you can't fault  the factory at least  i'm here  uh you know so i'm so grateful for that  tens of thousands of australians have  been stranded overseas since the  government closed its borders in march  last year caps on passenger numbers  coming into australian airports also  caused problems for those wanting to  return home during the pandemic it feels  really good i'm actually here for my  dad's funeral  so i've been trying to come home for an  year and a half  for now flights are limited to  australian citizens permanent residents  and immediate families who are now free  to travel without a permit but that  doesn't apply to tourists visa holders  and temporary residents  even when australians do get home travel  across the country is still a challenge  quarantine restrictions still apply in  states like queensland where i am and  the borders are still closed in western  australia to those states and  territories they consider high risk like  victoria and new south wales even the  act which is classified as a low-risk  jurisdiction susie keady is an  australian living in hong kong she is  due to fly into sydney in two weeks time  but she says her travel plans are  complicated by different rules in each  state  it's absolutely a logistical challenge  you know my family has spread across  uh new south wales south australia and  queensland so  you know  so firstly you know getting across the  international border um you know that  was sort of hurdle number one and now  having to navigate the different  restrictions and the different  approaches  to domestic borders um you know remains  challenging  that's a hurdle these reunited families  are willing to overlook for now  sarah clark al jazeera queensland  australia

